EXT. CATTLE GRID BRIDGE OVER DRY RIVERBED - DAWN

A rickety wooden bridge spanning a dry wash. A single telegraph pole leans beside it, wires humming. The sun is a pale coin over the mesa.

MARSHAL AMBROSE DRUMMOND-ASANTE (50s) stands at the center of the bridge with his rifle slung. He watches the horizon. Fifteen yards behind him, a woman in a worn blue dress sits on a crate, her wrists bound in front of her: AUGUSTINA "GUSSIE" RAINWATER-OKORO (30s). Neither of them speaks.

The telegraph key clatters on the pole's receiver box, a small brass box bolted to the wood. Ambrose crosses to it, listens, and writes in a notebook.

AMBROSE (V.O.)
(reading the wire)
Marshal Drummond. Judge arrives Thursday. Hold prisoner at bridge. Stop.

AMBROSE
(to himself)
Thursday. It's Monday.

GUSSIE
Marshal?

AMBROSE
Quiet.

GUSSIE
I'm only asking.

AMBROSE
Ask quietly.

He tears the page from the notebook and tucks it into his vest. A dust plume rises on the far side of the wash. A single rider, coming fast.

GUSSIE
Who's that?

AMBROSE
I don't know.

The rider reins in at the bridge, a woman in a dusty brown duster, her hat pulled low: DEPUTY PRUDENCE LOCKHART-ADEBOYE (30s). She dismounts and approaches, hands visible.

PRUDENCE
Marshal. Sorry I'm late.

AMBROSE
You're not late. You're not expected.

PRUDENCE
Judge Hollis sent me. He wants her at the courthouse by noon.

AMBROSE
The wire says Thursday.

PRUDENCE
Wire's wrong.

Gussie looks from one to the other, her face unreadable. The wires hum.

AMBROSE
Show me the order.

Prudence hands over a folded paper. He reads, nods slowly, and lets out a breath.

AMBROSE (CONT'D)
Judge's hand. All right.

He unties Gussie's wrists himself. She rubs them, flexing her fingers.

GUSSIE
I told you I'd be no trouble.

AMBROSE
You've been trouble since Sunday.

She smiles, almost. Prudence leads the horses forward and the three of them start across the bridge.
